/**
 * Auto-generated from the user-provided `chord-fingers.csv` dataset — do not hand-edit.
 *
 * Each tuple is `[root, type, frets]`:
 *  - `root`: the chord's root exactly as spelled in the source CSV (e.g. 'A#', 'Bb', 'Cb' are
 *    each kept as their own distinct entry, not merged with their enharmonic twin).
 *  - `type`: the chord-quality code exactly as spelled in the source CSV (e.g. 'maj', 'm7',
 *    '7(#9)', 'm(maj7)') — see `CHORD_TYPE_LABELS` in `chordLibrary.ts` for the human-readable name.
 *  - `frets`: the six fret positions, string 1 (low E) through string 6 (high e), comma-separated,
 *    'x' for a muted string, '0' for open — straight from the CSV's FINGER_POSITIONS column.
 *
 * `chordLibrary.ts` turns these into `FretShape`s: it derives each note's role (root/third/fifth/
 * other) and the diagram's fret range itself, by the same pitch-class arithmetic already used
 * for the rest of the app's chord data — it does not trust the CSV's own NOTE_NAMES/CHORD_STRUCTURE
 * columns, which turned out to disagree with the actual fretted notes on the large majority of rows
 * (most likely because real jazz-chord voicings often omit or substitute textbook scale degrees).
 * Exact-duplicate voicings for the same (root, type) were dropped, and each group's voicings are
 * ordered by fret position (lowest first).
 */

